Z||000005||ejayerik||12-11-2001||12:45 AM||||I'm guessing nobody has ever seen one in a Fiero then. That is a shame. Wouldn't it be nice to floor it and have so much power to the rear wheels that all you can do it circles till the rubber is burned off the tires? lol||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||12-11-2001||12:57 AM||||There are plenty of engine swaps with just as much stock power but much more affordable and easier to do.  The Northstar, Caddy 4.9, Supercharged 3800, and SBC 350 are all instant thoughts.  Plenty of power, perhaps a little heavier.  The TT Rotary is not only very very expensive to get a complete one, but would also require a lot of custom work all the way from adapting it to fit a transverse transaxle to building custom exhaust and cooling components.  Just too much work for what you get.<P>Bragging rights you'd have, common sense you wouldn't.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||fieroX||12-11-2001||02:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ejayerik:<BR><B>I'm guessing nobody has ever seen one in a Fiero then. That is a shame. Wouldn't it be nice to floor it and have so much power to the rear wheels that all you can do it circles till the rubber is burned off the tires? lol</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ill be able to do that with my 3800 sc.  Along with pull the front tires off the ground at the track with slicks.  We dont need no rice in our cars.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif">  </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroX.com</A><BR>coming soon L67 powered 87 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||12-11-2001||10:01 PM||||I always wanted to try that in one of my Lancia Scorpions,or just an early Fiat X-19(1900lbs),would be fun at the track.||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroJoe||12-11-2001||10:43 PM||||Also, what's going to happen when you have to replace parts on it, I.E. apex seals?  The RX-7 boys are having problems getting these I hear...<P>Yes, no valvetrain to worry about redline is cool, but what about when the crankshaft thingy breaks, do you know how to rebuild a Wankel engine?  The engineering is really cool how it all works, but I imagine trying to fix it would be a PITA||||reg||1
Z||000010||Nashco||12-11-2001||11:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>Also, what's going to happen when you have to replace parts on it, I.E. apex seals?  The RX-7 boys are having problems getting these I hear...<P>Yes, no valvetrain to worry about redline is cool, but what about when the crankshaft thingy breaks, do you know how to rebuild a Wankel engine?  The engineering is really cool how it all works, but I imagine trying to fix it would be a PITA</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Joe, those are just rumors from those afraid of the motors.  That's like when people tell you, "Fieros, I heard they catch on fire all the time!"  Not much truth to it.  Yes, parts are a little harder to come by than your typical SBC 350, but they're out there.  Also, the "crankshaft thingy" would be VERY hard to break.  The rotary motor is a perfectly balanced engine; the "crankshaft thingy" on a rotary is not only much smaller than a piston driven motor, but is also subjected to much less abuse.<P>For some awesome pictures and basic stuff, check this link out:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.howstuffworks.com/rotary-engine.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.howstuffworks.com/rotary-engine.htm</A> <P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||Blacktree||12-12-2001||03:27 AM||||Another bonus would be the reduced weight of a rotary versus a piston engine. My best friend has an RX7 (non-turbo)... I got to poke around in the engine compartment and that engine is TINY. It's about the size of a motorcycle engine. Even with a turbo setup, it would probably weight half as much as a 2.8 V6.<P>I got to drive it, too. The Wankel engine doesn't have as much off-the-line pull as a piston engine (unless you rev it up and pop the clutch), but you can wind that thing up until it starts losing power from air starvation (about 9000RPM) without worrying about damaging the engine. At 4000RPM+ it generates some respectable power.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Posthumane||12-12-2001||01:42 PM||||I actually considered doing this a while back (you could find my post on it if you do a search) but after looking at the work required compared to the power output, I don't think it's worth it. Maybe if I could get my hands on one of those 900 HP 6 rotor ones that some english company was building, I might change my mind, but I think in terms of good power to weight ratio, something like a quad4 or 3.4TDC might be a better choice.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Pyric||12-12-2001||03:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> Yes, parts are a little harder to come by than your typical SBC 350, but they're out there.  Also, the "crankshaft thingy" would be VERY hard to break.  The rotary motor is a perfectly balanced engine; the "crankshaft thingy" on a rotary is not only much smaller than a piston driven motor, but is also subjected to much less abuse.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Maybe so... but in 1990 I twisted the crankshaft in my GT in two.  Thing was a BI-ACH to replace.  It was probably a manufacturing flaw though.<P>The guy at the dealership just scratched his head while looking at it and asked "exactly how did you manage to do that?"<BR><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieronegS.jpg">   <P><BR>"Never offend people with style when you can offend them with substance." <BR>--Sam Brown||||reg||1
Z||000014||stsmithgt||12-12-2001||07:37 PM||||It you go with an older rotory, there're shops out there(somewhere) that make the engine with three rotors instead of two. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I wonder how much that helps the lower rpms?  I've seen a lot of sand buggies go with the rotory.  I used to go to sand drags like the ones that were held up in Yuba City CA.  Talk about a screemer!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  The people that didn't know it was a rotory powered dragster thought the thing was about to blow up. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||GTFiero1||12-12-2001||09:32 PM||||the problem with rotary engines is tha they dont last as long as normal piston engines and they have more prplems and more expensive problems. thats why you dont see them in many cars. but they are good engines until some thing goes wrong <P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_3.jpg">   <BR>you gotta keep on keepin on...<BR>IM AOL: GTFiero &  Rumpdetective (dont ask)||||reg||1
